  "slug": "simulations-structural-6310",
  "summary": "Structure-based approaches now connect cervid PRNP and human missense variants to predicted condensation signatures, interfaces and conserved hydration sites.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is structure-based inference linking disease-associated sequence variation to structural features. A preprint localizes predicted condensation-related signatures to two C-terminal regions of the prion protein across cervid PRNP variants associated with chronic wasting disease. A second preprint applies AI-based structural modelling to 109,282 human protein interaction structures, characterizing interfaces and mapping pathogenic variants onto predicted interfaces. The published study examines conserved water molecule sites in PDB structures, reporting enrichment for pathogenic missense variants, and uses MD simulations of GCase to link disruption of these sites to pathogenicity. What is new is the identification of conserved hydration sites as a structural correlate of missense pathogenicity. The items differ in scope, from prion condensation to interactome-wide interfaces, so the shared element is the use of structure to contextualize variant effects rather than a common mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
